  <h1>Best Protein Powder for GLP-1 Users: A UK Buyer's Guide</h1>
  <div class="article-meta"><span>Updated July 2026</span><span>·</span><span>8 min read</span></div>

  <p>Ready-made shakes are convenient, but powder is cheaper per serving and easier to tailor to a shrunken appetite. Here's how to pick one that works with a GLP-1, not against it.</p>

  <h2 id="why-protein">Why protein matters more on a GLP-1</h2>

  <p>When you lose weight quickly, some of what you lose is muscle — unless you actively defend it. Studies of GLP-1 users consistently show that a meaningful share of total weight lost can be lean mass, and the two best defences are resistance exercise and adequate protein. The usual target quoted for people losing weight on these medications is somewhere between <strong>1.2 and 1.6g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day</strong> — for many people that's 90–120g, which is genuinely hard to hit when your appetite has been switched off.</p>

  <p>That's the whole case for powder. It's not magic, it's just a dense, low-volume way to get 25g of protein into a body that isn't interested in a chicken breast.</p>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <div class="callout__label">Worth Knowing</div>
    <p>"GLP-1 shakes" and "GLP-1 support powders" marketed with wellness claims are mostly ordinary protein products with a premium label. You don't need a GLP-1-branded powder — you need enough protein in a format you can tolerate.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="three-types">The three types, and who each suits</h2>

  <h3>1. Standard whey (concentrate or isolate)</h3>
  <p>The default for a reason: complete protein, high in leucine (the amino acid that drives muscle retention), cheap, and available everywhere. Whey <em>isolate</em> is worth the small premium if regular whey leaves you bloated — it has less lactose. The catch on a GLP-1 is texture: a thick, milky shake can feel like a wall when you're already full or queasy.</p>
  <p><strong>Good UK options:</strong> MyProtein Impact Whey Isolate, Bulk Pure Whey Isolate. Both regularly discounted; never pay full list price.</p>

  <h3>2. Clear whey</h3>
  <p>Whey isolate processed to mix like squash or juice — light, fruity, no milkiness at all. For GLP-1 users this is often the winner, because nausea and early fullness make creamy textures genuinely off-putting. A cold, sharp raspberry drink with 20g of protein in it is a much easier sell at 8am than a vanilla milkshake.</p>
  <p><strong>Good UK options:</strong> MyProtein Clear Whey, Bulk Clear Whey Isolate, Applied Nutrition Clear Whey (widely stocked in supermarkets now).</p>

  <h3>3. Plant-based blends</h3>
  <p>A lot of people find dairy harder to digest on tirzepatide or semaglutide — slowed stomach emptying doesn't play nicely with lactose for everyone. A pea + rice blend gives you a complete amino acid profile without any dairy. Texture is grittier than whey; blending with frozen banana or oat milk helps.</p>
  <p><strong>Good UK options:</strong> Form Performance Protein (premium but genuinely pleasant), Bulk Vegan Protein Powder, MyProtein Pea Protein Isolate (budget, single-ingredient).</p>

  <h2 id="label">What to check on the label</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Protein per serving — aim for 20–30g:</strong> Below 20g and you're using precious stomach space inefficiently.</li>
    <li><strong>Sugar — under 3g per serving:</strong> Sugary shakes can worsen nausea and add calories you didn't need.</li>
    <li><strong>Leucine — 2g+ per serving if listed:</strong> The key trigger for muscle protein synthesis while losing weight.</li>
    <li><strong>A scoop you can halve:</strong> On bad-appetite days, half a serving twice a day beats one full one you can't finish.</li>
    <li><strong>Third-party testing (Informed Sport or similar):</strong> A nice-to-have quality signal, essential if you compete in tested sport.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="tips">Practical tips from the community</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Serve it cold and thin.</strong> More liquid, more ice. Thick shakes are the enemy of a GLP-1 stomach.</li>
    <li><strong>Sip, don't chug.</strong> Downing 400ml in one go on slowed stomach emptying is a recipe for discomfort. Treat it like a drink to nurse over half an hour.</li>
    <li><strong>Front-load your day.</strong> Many users find appetite is least bad in the morning. A clear whey with breakfast banks 20g before the day gets difficult.</li>
    <li><strong>Rotate flavours.</strong> Flavour fatigue is real when a powder becomes a daily fixture. Buy small bags until you know what you can face repeatedly.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't replace every meal.</strong> Powder fills the gap between what you can eat and what you need — it isn't a licence to stop eating protein-rich food. Whole food brings fibre, micronutrients and chewing, all of which matter.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <div class="callout__label">Medical Note</div>
    <p>If you have kidney disease or have been given specific protein guidance by your care team, follow that advice rather than general targets. And if you're struggling to keep any food or fluid down on a GLP-1, that's a conversation for your prescriber, not a supplement problem.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="maths">Powder vs ready-made shakes: the maths</h2>

  <p>A 1kg bag of clear whey at £25–£30 gives you roughly 40 servings — about 70p each. Ready-made options run £2–£2.50 a bottle. If you're using one a day, powder saves £40–£50 a month, which over a year is a decent chunk of a month's medication cost. Ready-made still earns its place for handbags, desks and travel — we've ranked the best of those separately in our <a href="/food-protein-shakes-ranked">protein shakes guide</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="bottom-line">The bottom line</h2>

  <p>The best protein powder on a GLP-1 is the one you'll actually drink on a nauseous Tuesday. For most people that's a clear whey or a light plant blend, 20–30g a serving, mixed thinner than the tub suggests, sipped slowly. Everything else — brand, flavour, price — is preference.</p>

